# End-to-end EMULATOR test for the BIGTREETECH SKR mini E3 v3.0
# (STM32G0B1) in DETERMINISTIC TICK MODE. Boots klipper firmware under
# Renode against the skr-mini-e3-v3.0 pin map, completes klippy identify
# + clocksync, brings up the full MCU config (4 TMC2209s, 2 software-PWM
# heaters, 2 fans, endstops, 2 thermistors), reads the thermistors
# through ADC1, round-trips the four TMC2209 register files over the
# shared software UART, and exercises interactive gcode (M114/M105)
# against real firmware.
#
# This is the STM32G0 per-printer test and the direct successor of
# skr_mini_e3_v2.test (STM32F103). The TMC2209 multi-drop UART path is
# identical (four drivers share PC11 rx / PC10 tx, distinguished by
# uart_address 0/2/1/3; the reply is always master-addressed so
# renode_hooks.sw_uart serves every address from one shared register
# file), so this test reuses that hook unchanged. The new ground it
# covers is the G0 ADC: thermistors on ADC1 channel 0 (PA0, extruder)
# and channel 17 (PC4, bed), read end to end by M105 via
# test/emulator/stm32g0_adc_stub.py - a Python.PythonPeripheral that
# completes klipper's G0 ISR/CR/CHSELR/DR ADC handshake
# (src/stm32/stm32f0_adc.c). The G0/F0 ADC register layout is completely
# different from the F1/F4 ADC, and upstream Renode's Analog.STM32G0_ADC
# neither self-clears ADCAL the way the calibration busy-wait needs nor
# returns klippy-controllable values, so without the stub the firmware
# either spins in calibration or every heater reads a fixed value.
#
# As with the other per-printer tests, the gated path is MOTION-FREE:
# tick mode resolves the renode wall-clock-vs-virtual-time drift, but
# motion commands (homing) are delivered by klippy's wall-clock-paced
# serialqueue background thread, which under shared-host CPU contention
# can deliver a tightly-scheduled stepper command late and trip "Timer
# too close". The boot / config / TMC-init / ADC / DUMP_TMC / M114 / M105
# path schedules no such tight-lead commands and is deterministically
# green. The firmware is the generic serial-mode stm32g0b1 build
# (stm32g0b1.dict); the real board is USB-only but the STM32G0 Renode
# platform models no USB device, and the printer pin map this test
# exercises is independent of the host link.
DICTIONARY stm32g0b1.dict
CONFIG skr_mini_e3_v3.cfg
EMULATOR skr_mini_e3_v3.fixture.json
REQUIRES_EMULATOR  # deterministic-time lockstep needs the real backend

# Interactive gcode round-trip against real firmware in tick mode.
# M114 reports toolhead position; M105 reports temperatures, reading both
# thermistors through ADC1 end to end. Neither drives a stepper.
M114
M105
# Read back the live TMC register file over the multi-drop UART for the
# lowest and highest addressed drivers. DUMP_TMC reads every register
# (incl. IOIN) live, so it only produces a full dump if the firmware
# round-tripped the read datagrams for that driver's address.
DUMP_TMC STEPPER=stepper_x
DUMP_TMC STEPPER=extruder

# klippy connected, loaded the dictionary, and built the full config
# against real firmware.
EXPECT_LOG_CONTAINS "Loaded MCU 'mcu'"
EXPECT_LOG_CONTAINS "Configured MCU 'mcu'"
# DUMP_TMC dumped both ends of the address range (addr 0 / addr 3); the
# IOIN read returning the TMC2209 version byte (0x21) proves the
# multi-drop UART read path returned framed, CRC-valid responses.
EXPECT_LOG_CONTAINS "DUMP_TMC stepper_x"
EXPECT_LOG_CONTAINS "DUMP_TMC extruder"
EXPECT_LOG_CONTAINS "version=0x21"
# A broken UART round-trip surfaces as a TMC init failure or a direct
# read/write error; the deterministic tick clock must produce neither.
EXPECT_LOG_NOT_CONTAINS "failed to init"
EXPECT_LOG_NOT_CONTAINS "Unable to read tmc uart"
EXPECT_LOG_NOT_CONTAINS "Unable to write tmc uart"
EXPECT_LOG_NOT_CONTAINS "Internal error"
# A renode-side crash (wrong sysbus peripheral, hook callback error)
# kills serial; klippy then gets EOF and TMC init / identify time out.
EXPECT_LOG_NOT_CONTAINS "Got EOF when reading from device"
EXPECT_LOG_NOT_CONTAINS "failed to init: Timeout"
# With the deterministic clock the config / TMC / ADC path pushes no
# deadline into the firmware's past, so nothing shuts down. A broken ADC
# stub would surface as a flat-zero / out-of-range thermistor read.
EXPECT_LOG_NOT_CONTAINS "ADC out of range"
EXPECT_LOG_NOT_CONTAINS "Timer too close"
EXPECT_LOG_NOT_CONTAINS "Rescheduled timer in the past"
EXPECT_LOG_NOT_CONTAINS "Klippy is shutdown"
